{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# An issue link DELETED in Jira is never removed from the local ticket (inbound)\n\nFound while writing the row-11 inbound cell of the J11 mutation table\n(story 5200-e04e-246e-4aae), filed per that story's rule that a cell revealing a\nproduct defect stops and reports. THIS TICKET NOW CARRIES THE FIX (reparented under\nepic e369-a449-4773-48fb by operator ruling).\n\n## Mechanism\n\nThe inbound link differ is ADD-ONLY, by construction and by its own docstring.\n`inbound_differ._diff_links_inbound` opens with \"Reflect Jira issuelinks into rebar\nrelations. ADD-only.\" and closes with \"ADD-only (no REMOVE mutations)\"\n(src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_differ.py:380 and :396). It iterates\nthe CURRENT `fields.issuelinks` and emits an add per unseen link; nothing walks the\nlocal deps looking for one whose Jira counterpart has gone.\n\nSo the flow is asymmetric: the OUTBOUND direction does have a removal path\n(`outbound_links._diff_link_removals`, which emits\n`{\"action\": \"remove\", \"type\":..., \"to_key\":..., \"relation\":...}`,\noutbound_links.py:120-175, applied via `delete_issue_link`), while the inbound\ndirection has no counterpart. A link a human deletes in Jira stays on the rebar\nticket forever, and no pass ever reports it.\n\n## Why this is a gap and not merely a documented design choice\n\nStory 5200's acceptance criteria define a 14-row mutation table with an explicit\noracle per direction. Row 11's INBOUND oracle is \"that link is ABSENT\" from the\nlocal ticket. That row cannot pass while the differ is add-only, so the epic's\ncomprehensiveness claim is not reachable without either implementing the removal\nor amending the AC. This ticket IMPLEMENTS THE REMOVAL; the Approach below resolves\nthe evidence question (how we know the peer deleted a link rather than that we failed\nto observe it), which is the part that made this more than a guard removal.\n\nNote the add-only choice is not obviously safe either: the same docstring's\nidempotency logic already skips an emit when \"the counterpart is absent from the\nACTIVE local set\", i.e. the differ is willing to reason about absence — just not\nabout the absence of the LINK.\n\n## Expected failure\n\n`tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py::test_inbound_delete_link_round_trips`\nasserts the local dep is gone after the Jira link is deleted. It should go RED on\nthe harness until this is addressed.\n\n## Approach\n\nMirror the outbound removal path, but gate it — an inbound removal is a WRITE that destroys local\ndata, so the whole design problem is establishing that the peer really DELETED the link rather than\nthat we merely failed to see it.\n\n1. **A new `_diff_link_removals_inbound` in `inbound_differ.py`**, the mirror of\n   `outbound_links._diff_link_removals` (outbound_links.py:120-175). It walks the local `deps` and\n   emits `{\"action\": \"remove\", \"target_id\": <local id>, \"relation\": <local relation>}` — inbound\n   records carry `target_id` (a local id), not `to_key`. Build the OBSERVED peer link set through\n   `link_direction.resolve_inbound_link`, NOT from raw Jira keys, so both sides are compared in local\n   relation vocabulary; comparing raw keys makes an `inwardIssue`/`Blocks` link read as `depends_on`\n   while the local dep is `blocks`, producing a spurious removal of a link that is still there.\n\n2. **`apply_inbound_records._inbound_update_apply_links` must branch on `action`.** It currently reads\n   `if entry.get(\"action\") != \"add\": continue` (apply_inbound_records.py:560-562) and only ever calls\n   `rebar.link`, so a removal record is silently discarded — a differ-only fix would ship the exact\n   silent-failure class this epic exists to end. Removal applies via `rebar.unlink`.\n\n### The five guards, and why each is load-bearing\n\n- **G1 OBSERVED.** Emit only when `\"issuelinks\" in jira_fields` — key PRESENT, empty list allowed.\n  `fetcher.py:592-596` sets that key only for entries `get_issuelinks_map` returned a list for, and the\n  base search omits issuelinks entirely (bug 3f04), so key-presence is a reliable \"enrichment reached\n  this issue\" marker. Both readers make an empty list authoritative rather than dropping the key\n  (Cloud `acli_graph.get_issuelinks_map` records `result[key] = links` for any list \"including the\n  empty list — an authoritative 'no links'\"; DC `transport.py:391-394` builds `{key: list(... or [])}`).\n  `_snapshot_schema.py` is `total=False` and never injects `[]`. So key-absent covers exactly the\n  unsafe cases — enrichment failed, HTTP 410, a fail-open partial map, the issue fell outside a\n  truncated page, a cross-project bound issue, or a client with no `get_issuelinks_map` — and every one\n  of those fails safe to NO REMOVAL.\n  **`_diff_links_inbound:397` opens `jira_fields.get(\"issuelinks\") or []`, which collapses\n  observed-empty into unobserved. That line must NOT be reused on the removal path.**\n- **G2 TARGET BOUND.** Require `binding_store.get_jira_key(target_id)` non-None. An unbound target\n  could never have had a Jira link, so its absence proves nothing.\n- **G3 MANAGED.** `should_propagate_removal(relation, target_id, local_ticket)` from\n  `rebar.reducer._managed_refs` — the same predicate the outbound parent/link gates consume, so a\n  peer-created dep rebar never owned is not clobbered. It also excludes\n  `duplicates`/`supersedes`/`discovered_from` for free, because `MANAGED_REF_KINDS` omits relations\n  with no reliable peer link type; do NOT add a redundant filter for them.\n- **G4 ARBITRATION — REWRITTEN AFTER THE LIVE ORACLE REFUTED THE FIRST VERSION. READ THIS CAREFULLY.**\n  The FIRST version of this guard said: \"never mirror a removal for a target the outbound side is adding\n  this pass\", on the reasoning that an outbound ADD means \"being pushed\" rather than \"peer deleted it\".\n  **That reasoning is WRONG, and its failure is total.** When the peer deletes a link, the local dep still\n  exists and the peer lacks it — which is EXACTLY the condition under which the outbound differ emits a\n  link ADD. So on any bidirectional pass a peer deletion always produces an outbound ADD for that target,\n  and the guard therefore always suppressed the inbound REMOVE: **the feature could never fire.** Proven by\n  harness run 30784592232 against main @ `de2f1ed87` (which CONTAINS change 1276):\n  `test_inbound_delete_link_round_trips` stayed RED with \"the Jira link was DELETED … but the local dep …\n  still targets 'jira-rbjflfv-2'\". The mutation ledger was green throughout, because\n  `test_g4_outbound_add_this_pass_suppresses_the_removal` asserted the behaviour the plan specified — **a\n  mutation ledger cannot catch a wrong SPECIFICATION.**\n\n  **THE CORRECTED RULE: `managed_refs` decides PRECEDENCE between the two directions. It is not a second\n  AND-gate beside G3.** The two differs disagree about one fact — local dep present, peer link absent — and\n  exactly one must win:\n    * ref **IS managed** (rebar pushed it at some point) + peer lacks it → **the peer DELETED it** → the\n      inbound REMOVE wins, **and the outbound ADD for that pair must be suppressed.**\n    * ref is **NOT managed** (never pushed) + peer lacks it → **we simply have not pushed it yet** → the\n      outbound ADD wins and the inbound REMOVE is suppressed. This is the ONLY case the old G4 should have\n      covered.\n  Suppressing only the inbound side is NOT sufficient and must not be shipped: both differs run against\n  the same pre-pass snapshot and then both apply, so inbound unlinking locally while outbound re-adds in\n  Jira leaves the pass DIVERGENT and the next pass re-adopts the link inbound — churn, not convergence.\n  **So `outbound_links.py` (or the shared suppression context) is in scope: this is arbitration across BOTH\n  differs.**\n  An outbound REMOVE for the same pair still suppresses the inbound removal (the two directions must not\n  both act on one pair in a pass; deferring re-evaluates next pass rather than double-removing).\n- **G5 RELATION MATCH AT APPLY.** `rebar.unlink(id1, id2, *, repo_root=None)` takes NO relation\n  argument, and that is deliberate — `_commands/unlink.py`'s usage text says \"pair-scoped, NO relation\n  arg; removes the most-recent link between the pair\", and `_get_link_info` returns the most recent\n  net-active link. So if a pair holds two active relations and the peer dropped only one, a naive\n  unlink can remove THE WRONG ONE — a link the peer still has. The apply step must confirm the pair's\n  net-active relation MATCHES the vanished relation, and skip + log otherwise.\n\n### G3's blind spot, stated plainly rather than papered over\n\n`add_managed_ref` is folded by the **LINK-event** processor (`reducer/_processors.py:411`), so a ref\nbecomes \"managed\" the instant it is created LOCALLY — it does NOT mean \"we pushed it to the peer\".\n`seed_managed_refs_from_current` compounds this for pre-feature tickets by seeding all current `deps`\nas managed. So G3 alone does not establish the peer ever had the link, and the residual hazard is: a\nlocal dep with a bound target and a mappable relation that outbound did NOT push this pass. G4 closes\nthat in a healthy pass, because outbound emits a link ADD for exactly that dep and the suppression\ndrops the removal. The residual window is therefore a DEGRADED pass — outbound link diffing skipped,\ndisabled, or failed while inbound proceeds — and is recorded here as a known limitation.\n\n**Alternative rejected: compare against the previous peer link set** (link present in the prior Jira\nsnapshot, absent now = a real remote deletion). This is the textbook-correct signal and would retire\nG3's blind spot outright, but it is NOT available: as of `6cdd4b00d9` (\"Narrow persisted Jira snapshot\nto key membership\") `reconcile._write_prev_snapshot_key_set` persists `{jira_key: {}}` — key membership\nonly, carrying no fields and therefore no issuelinks. Reaching for it means adding a new persisted\nartifact with its own migration and its own truncation semantics, which is a materially larger change\nthan this defect warrants. Recorded so a future reader knows it was considered and why it was declined.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_differ.py` — the removal differ + G1/G2/G3, and making\n  G4 deliberate. 718/800 LOC today; a ~50-line mirror lands ~770. If it would breach 800, shed weight\n  into `link_direction.py` (59 LOC, already imported by both directions), never mechanically.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py` — action branch + `rebar.unlink` + G5.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_links.py` (or the shared same-pass suppression context) —\n  **ADDED after the live refutation:** the managed case must also suppress the OUTBOUND link ADD, or the\n  pass ends divergent and churns. This is the arbitration half the original plan did not contemplate.\n  579/800 LOC.\n- `src/rebar/reducer/_managed_refs.py` — OPTIONAL, only to add a `should_mirror_peer_removal` sibling\n  sharing the `managed_ref_set()` read path. Mechanically the identical predicate, so it buys naming\n  and a place to document that inbound needs more than the managed check. Skip it if the guards read\n  clearly without it.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_inbound_link_removal_2b16.py` (new).\n- **Out of scope:** any fetcher or adapter change (the observed/unobserved distinction already\n  survives); `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py`, which another ticket owns.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a link deleted in Jira is mirrored locally: the local dep is asserted POSITIVELY ABSENT after\n      the pass, never \"no exception was raised\"\n- [ ] the removal record survives the apply layer end to end — differ emit → `run_differs` payload →\n      `apply_inbound` → `rebar.unlink` → the dep is gone from the reduced ticket. A test that stops at\n      the differ boundary does NOT satisfy this, because the record was silently dropped at\n      `apply_inbound_records.py:560-562` and a differ-only oracle goes green while the store is untouched\n- [ ] G1: with the issue's `issuelinks` key ABSENT (unobserved — simulate the real truncation/fail-open\n      shape, not a hand-deleted key if the real path differs), NO removal is emitted\n- [ ] G1: with `issuelinks` present and EMPTY (authoritative \"no links\"), the removal IS emitted\n- [ ] G2: an unbound target emits no removal\n- [ ] G3: a dep that is not in `managed_refs` emits no removal, so a peer-created dep is never clobbered\n- [ ] G4 arbitration, MANAGED case: a MANAGED dep whose peer link is gone emits the removal **even\n      though the outbound side is adding that target this pass**, and the outbound ADD for that pair is\n      SUPPRESSED. Assert BOTH halves — inbound removal present AND outbound add absent — because\n      suppressing only one leaves the pass divergent and churning\n- [ ] G4 arbitration, UNMANAGED case: an UNMANAGED dep whose peer link is absent emits NO removal and the\n      outbound ADD proceeds, asserted against `link_add_keys`\n- [ ] a test pins that the two arbitration branches are decided by `managed_refs` and NOT by the mere\n      presence of an outbound add — i.e. flipping only the managed-ness of the ref flips which direction\n      wins. This is the assertion whose absence let the first version ship broken\n- [ ] the live cell `test_inbound_delete_link_round_trips` is GREEN on a harness run, and the run id is\n      recorded here. **A green unit suite is explicitly NOT sufficient for this ticket** — the first\n      version had a fully green, mutation-checked unit suite and did not work at all\n- [ ] G5: when the pair's net-active relation differs from the vanished relation, no unlink is\n      performed and the skip is logged\n- [ ] steady state does not churn: a managed dep still present on the peer emits NO removal on\n      repeated passes\n- [ ] every oracle is MUTATION-CHECKED with verdicts recorded on this ticket, and the mutation set\n      MUST include making the removal unconditional — a fix that emits removals with no guard passes\n      the happy-path oracle while being a data-loss bug, so the guard oracles are the ones that\n      actually protect the product\n- [ ] `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py::test_inbound_delete_link_round_trips` is\n      unmodified, and turns GREEN on the harness once this lands\n- [ ] `inbound_differ.py` and `apply_inbound_records.py` each stay under the 800-LOC cap"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785768491521206001,"uuid":"0e2e22e5-228d-44c2-940f-c91916b57dbb"}